{"step_index":824,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T13:56:36Z","content":"The capture on **`th2-revproxyp-fe1`** has finished. Here is the exact result:\n\n```text\nlistening on any, link-type LINUX_SLL2 (Linux cooked v2), snapshot length 262144 bytes\n\n0 packets captured\n1 packet received by filter\n0 packets dropped by kernel\n```\n\n---\n\n### What This Proves (Root Cause)\n\n**`0 packets` reached the reverse proxy at the network layer.** \n\nThis means the failure is **not** an Nginx configuration issue, **not** an SSL certificate issue, and **not** an application error. The TCP handshake never arrives at `th2-revproxyp-fe1`.\n\n---\n\n### Why Did 0 Packets Arrive?\n\nDepending on what was entered in the field during your test:\n\n#### Scenario A: The field contained `https://109.62.64.18:9443` (as in Screenshot 2)\n* When the backend (`172.30.198.49`) tries to connect to `109.62.64.18`, it bypasses `/etc/hosts` (because it's an explicit IP) and sends the packet to the default gateway firewall (`172.30.198.54`).\n* As we discovered earlier, **the firewall does not have Hairpin NAT enabled**. It drops internal packets targeting its own public VIP `109.62.64.18`. The reverse proxy never sees the packet.\n\n#### Scenario B: The button is triggered by your Browser (Client-Side)\n* When you click \"Test connection to server\", the browser itself (on your workstation) tries to open a connection to `109.62.64.18:9443`.\n* Because your workstation is inside the company network / VPN, your connection to the public IP is also subject to the corporate firewall, which blocks or drops internal-to-public-VIP traffic.\n\n---\n\n### What URL was in the field when you clicked the button?\n\n1. Was it **`https://map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443`** or **`https://109.62.64.18:9443`**?\n2. If you press **F12** in your browser and click the button:\n   * Does the **Console** tab show an error like `ERR_CONNECTION_TIMED_OUT` or `CORS blocked` to `109.62.64.18:9443`?","thinking":"**Investigating Lack of Reverse Proxy Traffic**\n\nZero packets reaching the reverse proxy suggests a fundamental connectivity issue between the Central backend and the reverse proxy.  The \"Test connection\" action should originate from the Central backend, targeting the reverse proxy. Examining the path the test connection takes will reveal where the connection is failing.\n\n\n**Analyzing Connection Attempts & NAT**\n\nCentral's connection target is directly the public IP address, bypassing hostname resolution from `/etc/hosts`.  This means traffic from Central to the public IP will be blocked by the firewall due to the lack of hairpin NAT. Alternatively, if initiated from a user's browser, the same hairpin NAT issue exists. Consequently, either scenario results in connection failure and zero packets reaching the reverse proxy.\n\n\n"}
